{"id": 4396454, "name": "Reed v. Railroad Company", "name_abbreviation": "Reed v. Railroad Co.", "decision_date": "1892-01-16", "docket_number": "No. 2825", "first_page": "601", "last_page": "602", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "35 S.C. 601"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [], "analysis": {"cardinality": 131, "char_count": 1566, "ocr_confidence": 0.56, "sha256": "283eaf4b1ad03912c45eae46c5ad5edc8664ef14d08778c0bc0316bf2f25ac12", "simhash": "1:422b7c52d14b6624", "word_count": 262}, "last_updated": "2023-07-14T15:08:39.630048+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["Reed v. Railroad Company."], "opinions": [{"text": "This was a motion to dismiss an appeal on the grounds that no notice of appeal had been given, and no \u201cCase\u201d and exceptions made and served, nor printed copies thereof furnished to respondent after demand.\nIt seems that the Circuit Judge granted an order on June 4, 1891, directing \u201cthat the demurrer be sustained and the complaint dismissed with costs.\u201d Plaintiff gave notice on June 11 of her intention to appeal from this order, but judgment on the order was not formally entered up by the clerk of court until June 18. Respondent contended that appeal could not be taken from a mere order for judgment and that no appeal had been taken from the judgment itself.\nW. H. Mtzsimons, for the motion.\nMordeeai & Gadsden, contra.\nJanuary 16, 1892.\nAppellant\u2019s proposed \u201cCase\u201d served upon respondent omitted to set out in full the papers proposed to be incorporated into the \u201cCase.\u201d Thus: \u201cThe complaint is as follows: (Insert complaint, omitting title).\u201d Respondent proposed no amendments and appellant printed his \u201cBrief\u201d for the appeal, putting in the several papers in full at the places designated in his proposed \u201cCaseand three printed copies were served on respondent. Respondent thereupon demanded three printed copies of the \u201cCase\u201d as served upon him; but no further service was made. Respondent contended that the \u201cCase\u201d as served upon him in manuscript was no sufficient \u201cCase and if it was, then it should have been printed and served just as it was when served in manuscript.\nThe motion was refused.", "type": "majority", "author": null}], "attorneys": ["W. H. Mtzsimons, for the motion.", "Mordeeai & Gadsden, contra."], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "No. 2825.\nReed v. Railroad Company.\nNovember Term, 1891."}, "file_name": "0601-01", "first_page_order": 611, "last_page_order": 612}